          "type": "turn",
          "question": "If a clinician writes diabetes in one place and cataract in another, does the \"with\" convention connect them?",
          "answer": "Not based on what I could find. The retrieved passages do not say whether cataract is one of the pairings the coding index links to diabetes, so I can't confirm the convention applies here.\n\nThe \"with\" convention is described in the coding summary \"Coding type 2 diabetes: E11 and its complication subcategories.\" It says that where the index links two conditions with \"with,\" the coder reads this as \"associated with\" or \"due to\" and presumes the relationship, without needing the record to state it. Another document, \"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection,\" adds two limits. The presumption holds only for pairings the index actually lists, and it is overridden if the documentation says the two conditions are unrelated.\n\nBoth passages describe a single record that lists both conditions. Neither says how separate documents from different clinicians are handled, so I can't say whether they would be linked.\n\nTo check this, a coder would need the alphabetic index entry for diabetes and the documentation itself, which were not in these results. Your clinician or a medical coder can tell you how a specific record would be coded.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
